Files (both in /=EC=9E=90=EB=A3=8C):
- WFMK202607.pdf =3D World Fleet Monitor, Vol.17 No.7 (Clarkson)
- Shipping Review and Outlook_07 2026.pdf =3D Shipping Review & Outlook, July=
 2026 (Clarkson)

Buyer-side data:
WFMK202607.pdf, p.21, table "Buyers of Secondhand Vessels", South Korea row (=
ranked #1 of "Top Countries/Regions"):
2025 full year =3D 39 ships / 3,226 thousand GT
2026 YTD (Jan-Jun) =3D 70 ships / 10,180 thousand GT, UP +531.1% y/y -- world=
's No.1 buyer this period

No Clarkson forecast exists for 2027 S&P. "Normalization scenario, ~3,000-5,0=
00 thousand GT, risk of a sharp drop" is my own call, based on:

1) The spike is mostly one buyer's one-off fleet consolidation
Shipping Review and Outlook, July 2026, p.34-35, Section 1.5 "Sale & Purchase=
":
"The tanker S&P market saw exceptionally strong volumes in Q1. Sinokor's VLCC=
 fleet expansion push made it the sector's largest buyer..."
"VLCC S&P had an especially active start to the year; South Korea's Sinokor (=
reportedly backed by MSC) continued the fleet consolidation push it started i=
n Q4-25, acquiring 51 VLCCs by early Apr (plus 13 in Dec-25)." -> 51+13 =3D 6=
4 VLCCs
"Even without the Sinokor transactions, though, activity levels were healthy =
(total VLCC sales: 2x 10yr avg; sales ex. Sinokor: +10%)."
-> i.e. strip Sinokor out and VLCC S&P growth is only about +10% y/y, not +53=
1%. Most of the Korea headline number is one buyer's consolidation deal, not =
broad-based owner activity.

2) Simply doubling H1 assumes the one-off deal repeats
10,180 x 2 =3D 20,360 thousand GT annualized -- this bakes in Sinokor-scale b=
uying continuing indefinitely, which is unlikely once that consolidation push=
 is done.

3) Caution on the "eco-secondhand demand" driver -- this one is less settled =
than I first assumed
Same report, p.44-45, Section 1.6 "Fuelling Transition" (re: the IMO's mid-te=
rm carbon measure, officially the "Net Zero Framework"/NZF):
"However, deep divisions between member states on the NZF have become apparen=
t, with a delay to the vote for adoption in Oct-25 following strong lobbying =
from the US and Saudi Arabia. It is unclear whether the framework will be ado=
pted and, if it is adopted, what form it will take."
-> As of this July-2026 report, the NZF (the "2027 carbon levy") is NOT confi=
rmed and its timing is uncertain. Should not lean on this as a settled 2027 d=
river for eco-secondhand demand.

So: 2025 baseline 3,226 thousand GT, 2026 H1 10,180 (~64 VLCCs bought by one =
buyer driving most of it, underlying market ex-Sinokor only +10%) -> once tha=
t one-off deal effect fades, assume 2027 normalizes back toward roughly the 2=
025 level, maybe a bit above it (3,000-5,000 range) if the modest underlying =
growth continues -- flagged as a real risk of a sharp drop, not a base case o=
f continued Sinokor-scale buying.

